  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday morning through next Monday)\nIssued at 301 PM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nAs we approach the weekend, the ridge will begin to break down and \nsoutherly flow will begin to pull moisture back into the CWA. This \nwill bring chances for thunderstorms back up to between 25-35% \nduring the afternoon on Friday with more widespread thunderstorms \nlikely through the weekend. Temperatures should remain moderated to \nthe upper 80s and lower 90s, though that still has them running 5-10 \ndegrees above normal for this time of year. \n\nWe'll need to keep an eye on Sunday, as the best dynamics move \nthrough the area for stronger thunderstorms. The ECMWF builds in \nplenty of instability and decent low level shear, though the GFS \ntends to keep a majority of the bulk shear off to the north. \nRegardless of severe weather, the concern for flash flooding will \npersist through the weekend and into early next week before the \nfront finally clears out. The amount of rain we get will likely \nchange as the forecast window gets closer, but the NBM is calling \nfor up to 5 inches total from Friday to late Tuesday; mainly along \nand north of I-85.",
